     "type": "intro",
     "title": "The 150 Limit",
     "content": "British anthropologist Robin Dunbar spent decades studying primate social groups and found a striking pattern: across primate species, the size of the neocortex, the brain region responsible for complex social cognition, correlates with the maximum size of a stable social group that species can maintain. Applying the same ratio to the human neocortex, Dunbar calculated a predicted upper limit of roughly 150 stable relationships, the number of people whose individual identities, social obligations, and history with us we can realistically track well enough to trust them the way we trust family. What makes the number compelling is not just the math but how often it shows up independently across human history and culture: the typical size of hunter-gatherer bands and Neolithic farming villages, the basic fighting unit in many historical armies, the average size of a Hutterite religious colony before the group deliberately splits in two, and the headcount many modern companies informally notice as a threshold where the culture 'changes.' Below roughly 150, most members can plausibly know who everyone is, understand how they fit into the group, and extend trust based on direct or one-degree-removed personal knowledge. Above that threshold, an individual can no longer track the whole group from memory, and trust has to be mediated by something other than personal familiarity, whether that is formal rules, hierarchy, reputation systems, or deliberate culture-building. This lesson explores why that transition matters enormously for how leaders design organizations.",
