     "type": "concept",
     "title": "Why the Line at 150 Matters for Organizations",
     "content": "Dunbar's number is not simply a piece of trivia; it marks a real structural transition in how trust can function. Below approximately 150 people, an organization can run substantially on personal relationships: a founder can plausibly know every employee's name, role, and something about their life, and that direct familiarity does a great deal of the work that formal management systems would otherwise have to do, like catching problems early, keeping people honest, and building loyalty. Once an organization crosses that threshold, no single person, no matter how gifted a leader, can hold the whole group in personal working memory. Something has to fill the gap left by personal familiarity, and organizations tend to fill it in one of two ways. The unhealthy path is to fill the gap almost entirely with formal control: rules, surveillance, rigid hierarchy, and metrics-based management substituting for trust, which tends to produce exactly the disengagement and self-protective behavior described in earlier lessons on cortisol and insecure workplaces. The healthier path, and the one this lesson focuses on, is to deliberately re-create smaller trust-sized units inside the larger structure, so that most employees still experience an actual Circle of Safety of manageable size, typically a direct team, department, or facility well under 150, even though the parent organization numbers in the thousands. The leader's job in a large organization is not to personally maintain ten thousand relationships; it is to design the structure so that everyone belongs to some sub-group where Dunbar-scale trust is still possible.",
