     "type": "example",
     "title": "A Leader Who Practices Empathy Deliberately",
     "content": "Consider a plant manager who does not consider herself naturally warm but has decided to treat empathy as a skill to train, the way she once trained to become a certified engineer. She blocks fifteen minutes at the start of each shift for what she calls a no-agenda walk: she moves through the floor with no clipboard and no item to check off, simply greeting people and asking open questions, and she deliberately resists the urge to solve problems during this window even when someone mentions one, saving that for a separate conversation. Before difficult one-on-one conversations, she spends two minutes beforehand explicitly writing down what she imagines the other person is worried about walking in, a small perspective-taking exercise she treats as seriously as any pre-meeting prep. She has a standing rule that phones stay in a drawer during one-on-ones, a presence practice she adopted after realizing she used to glance at hers during difficult conversations without noticing. None of this came naturally at first, and she describes her first year of no-agenda walks as awkward and effortful, closer to learning a new physical skill than expressing a personality she already had. Two years in, employees describe her as one of the most approachable leaders in the company, not because her underlying temperament changed, but because the specific practices she repeated thousands of times became close to automatic, exactly the way any other well-rehearsed discipline does.",
