     "title": "Reading Popular Business Books Like a Critical Scholar",
     "content": "None of these critiques mean Leaders Eat Last should be discarded; they mean it should be read the way a college-level thinker reads any influential, evidence-adjacent popular text, as a useful entry point and organizing metaphor rather than a settled scientific conclusion. A few habits make this kind of critical consumption practical. First, treat vivid mechanisms, like a specific chemical explanation for a workplace feeling, as a hypothesis worth testing against your own experience and other frameworks, not as a proven fact simply because it is stated with confidence and a scientific-sounding vocabulary. Second, actively seek out at least one rigorously tested academic framework covering the same territory, such as Self-Determination Theory for motivation or Edgar Schein's models of organizational culture, and notice where it agrees with the popular account and where it adds nuance or complicates a too-simple story. Third, separate the book's practical advice from its explanatory claims; a recommendation like giving specific public credit to employees can be genuinely good leadership advice regardless of whether the underlying mechanism is precisely serotonin or something more complex that current neuroscience cannot yet fully specify. Fourth, notice your own reaction to a tidy explanation; the appeal of a clean, memorable story is itself worth being suspicious of, since real organizational problems are rarely one chemical, one incentive, or one leadership behavior away from being solved, no matter how satisfying that idea feels.",
